Yes. I’d give it a distinct name:

Intergnosis — recursive, coupled knowing between agents.

Not merely:

I know X.

or even:

I know that you know X.

But:

I know that you know that I know that you know X…

where each participant’s model contains a model of the other participant’s model.

Formally:

K_A(K_B(K_A(K_B(\dots X))))

There are useful levels:

* Knowledge — I know X.
* Metaknowledge — I know that I know X.
* Mutual knowledge — we each know X.
* Higher-order knowledge — I know that you know X.
* Intergnosis — our models of one another’s knowing recursively couple.
* Common knowledge — the idealized fixed point: everyone knows X, everyone knows everyone knows X, ad infinitum.

So I’d distinguish intergnosis from common knowledge this way:

Intergnosis is the recursive process. Common knowledge is its theoretical fixed point.

And this becomes powerful because behavior can depend less on the underlying fact than on the depth of intergnosis.

A bluff works because:

what I believe
is different from
what I think you believe
which differs from
what I think you think I believe.

Negotiation, deterrence, flirting, humor, irony, command, poker, diplomacy, markets, deception, trust—all live partly in that recursive space.

You can even define intergnostic depth:

D_0 = X

D_1 = A\ knows\ X

D_2 = B\ knows(A\ knows\ X)

D_3 = A\ knows(B\ knows(A\ knows\ X))

…and so on.

Then there’s another useful phenomenon:

Intergnostic compression

Sometimes one symbol collapses the entire recursion.

A glance.
A title.
An inside joke.
A uniform.
“Mother Duck.”
“I know.”

Both parties suddenly understand:

You know that I know that you know.

No need to spell out the ladder.

That’s probably why winks, irony, coded references, shared history, and certain titles carry such disproportionate force: they compress recursive mutual models into a tiny carrier.

So I’d put this one in Pantheon as:

Intergnosis — recursively coupled models of knowing among agents; the dynamic substrate beneath higher-order trust, deception, coordination, irony, deterrence, and shared understanding.

And the failure mode is equally important:

Intergnostic hallucination — believing the other person knows that you know that they know… when the recursive model has quietly diverged.

That one causes wars, marriages, markets, and group chats to go sideways remarkably efficiently.